# 🐧 Linux Mint Command Reference

A collection of Linux commands I am learning and practicing while using Linux Mint.

---

## 📁 1. File & Directory Commands

### `pwd`

Shows your current directory.

```bash
pwd
```

### `ls`

Lists files and folders.

```bash
ls
ls -la
ls -lh
```

### `cd`

Changes directories.

```bash
cd Documents
cd ..
cd ~
```

### `mkdir`

Creates a new directory.

```bash
mkdir projects
```

### `touch`

Creates a new file.

```bash
touch notes.txt
```

### `cp`

Copies files or directories.

```bash
cp notes.txt backup.txt
```

### `mv`

Moves or renames a file.

```bash
mv notes.txt linux-notes.txt
```

### `rm`

Removes a file.

```bash
rm notes.txt
```

> ⚠️ Be careful with `rm`. Deleted files may not be recoverable.

---

# 📄 2. Viewing Files

### `cat`

Displays the contents of a file.

```bash
cat linux-notes.txt
```

### `less`

Views a file one screen at a time.

```bash
less linux-notes.txt
```

### `head`

Shows the beginning of a file.

```bash
head linux-notes.txt
```

### `tail`

Shows the end of a file.

```bash
tail linux-notes.txt
```

---

# 🔎 3. Searching

### `grep`

Searches for text inside files.

```bash
grep "Linux" linux-notes.txt
```

### `find`

Searches for files and directories.

```bash
find . -name "*.txt"
```

### `which`

Shows where a command is located.

```bash
which bash
```

---

# 🔐 4. Permissions

### `ls -l`

Displays file permissions.

```bash
ls -l
```

### `chmod`

Changes file permissions.

```bash
chmod +x script.sh
```

### `chown`

Changes file ownership.

```bash
sudo chown $USER:$USER file.txt
```

---

# 👤 5. User Commands

### `whoami`

Shows the current user.

```bash
whoami
```

### `id`

Displays user and group information.

```bash
id
```

### `groups`

Shows the groups your user belongs to.

```bash
groups
```

### `sudo`

Runs a command with administrator privileges.

```bash
